,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530764 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530752 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530752 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t + 1,t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530752 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d5a26a2-4d59-5e5b-a1a3-1666d9c373b2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530752 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2+4- \\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530755 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530757 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530755 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530755 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5742e24a-185d-570b-8c93-c58d8a73320b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530755 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- 3 y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- 3 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530765 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530738 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530765 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530765 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530765 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc21e937-2785-5c98-a85b-95973fde2ff7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530765 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(\\pi) & =\\langle \\pi,\\pi^{2}\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi,\\pi^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi,\\pi^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(0\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0- 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0-2\\right) \\\\  & =2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530767 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530768 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530767 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530767 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t - 2,t - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2766a0b-7719-5fca-8223-c9d01bd4c6c3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530767 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{3 y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle -2,-2\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle -1,-1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)-f(-2,-2) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}-6-6 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530778 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530769 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530769 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530769 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9033e6e-7d03-5cf3-9e4f-e08eff1e5a07" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530769 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fund